  "generated_feedback": "You've correctly identified that ending sounds are one way to determine the status of a word. However, your answer makes a significant error by stating that ending combinations are never used. The provided text shows that ending combinations are crucial for determining the status of pairs and plurals. It also provides an important rule: always check for ending combinations before you check for ending sounds. Please review the lesson material on pairs and plurals.",
  "rewritten_answer": "The status of a word in Arabic is determined by its ending. There are two types of endings: ending sounds (vowels) and ending combinations (letters added to the end). For singular words, status is generally shown by ending sounds: a ضمة indicates الرفع, a فتحة indicates النصب, and a كسرة indicates الجر. For pairs and plurals, status is shown by ending combinations. When determining status, you must always look for ending combinations before looking for ending sounds."
}"

